      "text": "# Round 2 Independent Review: Roadmap Explainability v2\n\n## Executive Position\n\nI would **not greenlight** this roadmap in its current form. While it demonstrates admirable architectural discipline in several areas (read-only constraints, schema-first thinking, phase gating), it contains **two fatal flaws** and **one systemic process failure** that make it unshippable:\n\n1. **Fatal: Privacy-by-hope-not-design** \u2014 PII redaction is sequenced *after* the user-facing surface ships\n2. **Fatal: Schema coupling masked as \"read-only\"** \u2014 the design assumes `.work-state.json` stability it cannot guarantee\n3. **Systemic: Council-pass-as-appendix** \u2014 the review mechanism that caught both flaws is sequenced to allow implementation before remediation\n\n---\n\n## 1. Points of Agreement with Prior Reviewers\n\n### \u2705 Reviewer A: Schema stability is wishful thinking (lines 85-92, 421-428)\n\n**Agreed without reservation.** The claim \"state.py v1 stays\" (line 91) is immediately contradicted by the council's own finding:\n\n> \"If the universal-engine bumps the schema (and it has, twice \u2014 see ADR-008 successors), the explain output breaks silently.\" (lines 421-422)\n\nThe roadmap treats `.work-state.json` as a stable API but provides no evidence. Reviewer A correctly identifies this as **blocking** and prescribes the right fix: pin a concrete schema version in Phase 1, not Phase 5.\n\n**Where I'd strengthen Reviewer A's critique:** The suggested fix (schema-version check at runtime) is *necessary but insufficient*. The deeper issue is that **no contract exists for `.work-state.json`**. The file is an implementation detail of `work_engine/state_io.py`, not a documented API. The roadmap should either:\n\n1. Promote `.work-state.json` to `docs/contracts/work-state.schema.json` (parallel to `explain-trace.schema.json`), OR\n2. Acknowledge the coupling as intentional technical debt and add a monitoring gate: if `state.py` changes, this roadmap's tests regress to red and must re-pass before merge.\n\nThe current draft does neither \u2014 it pretends the coupling doesn't exist.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Reviewer A: PII redaction sequencing is backwards (lines 411-416, Phase 5.2)\n\n**Agreed, and this is the roadmap's most dangerous flaw.** Phase 2 delivers `--json` output (lines 291-297) that can contain:\n\n- User prompts (from `state.input.data`)\n- File paths (from `state.assumptions[]`)\n- Council verbatims (from `council-responses.json`)\n\nPhase 5.2's Council TODO #4 (lines 411-416) adds redaction, but that TODO is **non-blocking** \u2014 the text says \"fold into the roadmap *or carve out with a one-line rationale*\" (line 438). This creates a three-week window where:\n\n1. Phase 2 merges \u2192 `explain last --json` ships\n2. User runs it \u2192 PII leaks to stdout \u2192 user pipes to Slack/CI \u2192 breach\n3. Phase 5 merges \u2192 redaction exists, damage already done\n\n**Reviewer A's fix (move redaction to Phase 2, Step 2.3) is correct.** I would add: make it a **Phase 1 prerequisite** to audit whether `scripts/ai_council/redact_low_impact_entry.py` (line 413) even exists. The roadmap cites it but never proves it's importable or tested.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Reviewer B: Rollback strategy is underspecified (lines 268-279)\n\n**Agreed.** The `explain.enable_last: false` flag (lines 268-279) is a correct kill-switch *pattern*, but the roadmap never defines **when to pull it**. Reviewer B correctly notes:\n\n> \"there's a lack of detailed rollback criteria or strategy for disabling this feature in case of severe issues after deployment\"\n\n**What's missing:** Add to Phase 2.4 a **rollback trigger list**:\n\n- If `explain last` writes to disk (violating the read-only constraint), disable by default\n- If any fixture's `--json` output fails schema validation post-merge, regress to draft\n- If user reports contain \"explain last leaked [PII pattern]\", disable fleet-wide and hotfix\n\nThe current draft treats the flag as a user preference, not an operational circuit breaker.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Points of Disagreement with Prior Reviewers\n\n### \u274c Reviewer A: `enable_last: false` flag is \"premature pessimization\" (lines 477-487)\n\n**I disagree with Reviewer A's pushback here.** Reviewer A argues:\n\n> \"The feature is read-only, makes no network calls, and reads artefacts already on disk. The cost is negligible. The only valid reason to disable is 'the state file doesn't exist,' which should exit 1.\"\n\n**Why this is wrong:**\n\n1. **Cost is not just compute.** On `cost_profile: minimal`, the *cognitive cost* of another subcommand matters. A CI script that runs `agent-config doctor && agent-config explain last && agent-config status` burns developer attention even if each command is cheap. The flag lets minimal-profile users opt out of explainability entirely if their workflow doesn't need it.\n\n2. **\"State file doesn't exist\" is not an error.** If the user hasn't run `/work` yet, there's no state to explain. Exiting 1 would break `make lint && agent-config explain last` chains. The roadmap correctly treats \"nothing to explain\" as exit 0.\n\n**Where Reviewer A is half-right:** The flag should default to `true` on all cost profiles *except minimal*, where it defaults to `false`. The current draft leaves this ambiguous (line 272: \"Default: `true` on every cost_profile\"). **Fix:** lines 268-279 should specify per-profile defaults.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Reviewer A: Council-pass sequencing is \"backwards\" (lines 359-372, 521-542)\n\n**I half-agree, but Reviewer A's prescription is too rigid.** Reviewer A proposes:\n\n> \"Phase 0: Council pass (before Phase 1 starts)\"\n\n**Why this would hurt more than help:**\n\nThe council can't review a schema that doesn't exist yet. Phase 1 (lines 137-168) *creates* `explain-trace.schema.json`. Asking the council to review it before it's drafted invites bikeshedding on empty space.\n\n**The real sequencing failure is narrower:** The council's **breaking findings** (PII redaction, schema versioning, URL hygiene, cost-metadata stripping) should block **Phase 2's merge**, not be appendixed as optional TODOs. The current gate (line 438) says:\n\n> \"All seven items above are folded into the roadmap *or carved out with a one-line rationale*\"\n\nThat \"or\" is the poison. **Fix:** Change Phase 5.2's resolution gate (lines 434-438) to:\n\n```diff\n- [ ] All seven items above are folded into the roadmap **or carved out with a one-line rationale**\n+ [ ] All seven items flagged as BLOCKING by any council member are resolved and tests added; \n+     items flagged INFORMATIONAL may be deferred with rationale\n```\n\nThen move the PII/schema items to Phase 2's exit gate. This preserves the \"council reviews the spec after Phase 1\" model but makes critical findings non-negotiable.\n\n---\n\n### \u274c Reviewer B: \"Cross-module dependencies\" on council/memory formats are unaddressed (section 1, para 2)\n\n**I disagree that this is a gap.** Reviewer B writes:\n\n> \"A potential risk exists if these modules have unaccounted updates that affect their output format, possibly leading to parsing errors\"\n\n**Why this is already handled:**\n\n- Council sessions: The roadmap explicitly constrains to files matching `mtime` inside the run window (lines 244-246). If the council emitter changes format, the parser reads the *old* format from the archived session. No risk of mid-run format switch.\n- Memory: The roadmap specifies `agents/metrics/skill-usage.jsonl` (line 247), which is an **append-only log**. Appending never breaks parsing. If the log format changes, the change is versioned (the repo already does this \u2014 `skill-usage.jsonl` has a `\"version\":2` field per entry).\n\n**Where Reviewer B is half-right:** The roadmap doesn't prove `council-responses.json` has a version field. **Add to Phase 2.2, `council.py` (line 244):**\n\n```diff\n- reads `<root>/agents/council-sessions/*/council-responses.json`\n+ reads `<root>/agents/council-sessions/*/council-responses.json`; if `version` field is absent or != 1, skips the file with a warning\n```\n\nBut this is a **refinement**, not a blocking flaw.\n\n---\n\n## 3. New Points Not Raised in Round 1\n\n### \ud83d\udd34 BLOCKING: The `--quiet` flag is spec'd but never gated (line 119, Phase 2.1)\n\n**Lines 217-218** add `--quiet` to the argparse surface:\n\n```python\n[--quiet]                # suppress the \"tip:\" footer\n```\n\nBut **nowhere in Phases 2-5** is there a test or exit gate requiring `--quiet` to work. The Phase 2 exit gate (lines 291-297) checks `--json` but not `--quiet`.\n\n**Why this matters:** If `--quiet` is broken, CI scripts that parse the output will fail. The \"tip:\" footer (whatever it says) will contaminate JSON or Markdown streams.\n\n**Fix:** Add to Phase 2 exit gate (line 297):\n\n```diff\n- [ ] `cost_profile: minimal` users can disable the subcommand via `explain.enable_last: false`\n+ [ ] `cost_profile: minimal` users can disable the subcommand via `explain.enable_last: false`\n+ [ ] `python3 -m scripts._cli.cmd_explain last --quiet` produces output with no \"tip:\" or \"hint:\" lines (test via `grep -i tip`)\n```\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd34 BLOCKING: The \"no recent run\" error message is underspecified (line 116)\n\n**Line 116** says:\n\n> `python3 -m scripts._cli.cmd_explain last` with no recent run returns exit 1 and a \"no recent run found; expected `.work-state.json` at \u2026\" message\n\n**The flaw:** The message says \"expected at \u2026\" but never specifies the path. Does it say:\n\n- `/absolute/path/to/project/.work-state.json` (bad: leaks directory structure in error logs)\n- `.work-state.json in current directory` (ambiguous if user ran from a subdirectory)\n- `.work-state.json at project root` (correct)\n\n**What I need to see:** Add to Phase 2 exit gate:\n\n```diff\n+ [ ] The \"no recent run\" message (line 116) prints a relative path from `--project-root`, never an absolute path\n```\n\nThis prevents PII leakage via error messages (usernames in home directories, etc.).\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f CONCERN: The renderer is spec'd as 150 LOC but does 7+ things (lines 255-267)\n\n**Phase 2.3** specifies:\n\n> `render.py` (NEW, \u2264 150 LOC). Pure-function `render(trace) -> str`.\n\nThen lists a Markdown template with **seven sections**: route, profile/preset table, memory, council, assumptions, halt (Phase 3), provider (Phase 3).\n\n**Why this is a hidden coupling risk:** 150 LOC for seven sections is 21 LOC/section. That leaves no room for:\n\n- Error handling (what if `trace.council` is malformed?)\n- Redaction (which Reviewer A correctly notes must happen in Phase 2)\n- Width-wrapping (Council TODO #1 says to use `script_output` helpers)\n- The section-registry pattern (Reviewer A's point about Phase 3 extensibility)\n\n**This is the single architectural call I'd push back on hardest.** The 150 LOC budget is **too tight** and will force Phase 3 to rewrite the renderer, breaking Phase 2's snapshot tests.\n\n**Evidence that would change my mind:**\n\nShow me an existing `scripts/_cli/cmd_*.py` file that renders structured output in <150 LOC. If `cmd_doctor.py` or `cmd_status.py` (both mentioned in Council TODO drift-lens) do this, the budget is feasible. If they're 200-300 LOC, this roadmap is underestimating by 50%.\n\n**Prescriptive fix:**\n\n1. Remove the LOC budget from line 255. It's a guess disguised as a constraint.\n2. Add to Phase 2 exit gate: \"Renderer passes `pylint` with no complexity warnings (`too-many-branches`, `too-many-statements`).\"\n3. Split `render.py` into `render.py` (orchestration) and `sections/` package (one module per section). Phase 3 adds new modules without editing `render.py`.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udfe1 OPEN QUESTION: What does \"one-line rationale\" mean in the council resolution gate? (line 438)\n\n**Line 438** says:\n\n> All seven items above are folded into the roadmap **or carved out with a one-line rationale**\n\n**The ambiguity:** Does \"one-line\" mean:\n\n- Literally one sentence? (60-80 characters)\n- One paragraph? (3-5 sentences)\n- One commit message? (unlimited, but single logical unit)\n\n**Why this matters:** If \"one-line\" is literal, complex rationales (like \"we defer URL hygiene because the terminal auto-link risk only affects terminals with custom link handlers, which is <1% of users per analytics\") get compressed into \"deferred \u2014 low impact,\" losing the reasoning.\n\n**Fix:** Replace \"one-line rationale\" with \"rationale \u2264100 words, added to the relevant phase's commentary.\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Single Architectural Call I'd Push Back Hardest On\n\n### \ud83d\uded1 **Making `--json` the canonical format (lines 217-218, Phase 2.1)**\n\nThe roadmap says:\n\n```python\n[--json]                 # emit ExplainTrace JSON instead of Markdown\n```\n\nThis implies **Markdown is the default**. But every other signal in the roadmap points the opposite way:\n\n- The schema (Phase 1.1, lines 139-157) is JSON-first\n- The unit tests (Phase 4.2, line 355) validate against JSON\n- The GUI roadmap (line 504) will consume `--json`, not Markdown\n- The lint surface (Phase 1.2, lines 160-165) validates JSON, not Markdown\n\n**The inversion:** The human-readable format (Markdown) is treated as primary, but the machine-readable format (JSON) is the one with a schema, tests, and downstream consumers.\n\n**Why this is backwards:**\n\n1. **Markdown has no contract.** If the renderer changes the section order or column names in Phase 3, nothing breaks. But if the JSON schema changes, `lint_explain_trace.py` (line 161) catches it. The schema provides mechanical sympathy; Markdown does not.\n\n2. **The `--json` flag is a trapdoor.** Users who pipe `explain last | grep \"Why\"` won't notice a format change until their script breaks. Users who pipe `explain last --json | jq` get a validation error immediately.\n\n3. **The roadmap's own tests are JSON-first** (line 295: `--json | jq -e '.version == 1'`). If Markdown were primary, the tests would parse Markdown and assert section presence.\n\n**Prescriptive fix:**\n\n1. **Flip the default.** Make `--json` the default output; add `--markdown` to opt into human-readable. Update line 217:\n\n   ```diff\n   - [--json]                 # emit ExplainTrace JSON instead of Markdown\n   + [--markdown]             # emit human-readable Markdown (default is JSON)\n   ```\n\n2. **Add a pager to the Markdown path.** If a user runs `agent-config explain last --markdown`, pipe the output through `less -R` (or respect `$PAGER`). This acknowledges that Markdown is for interactive browsing, not scripting.\n\n3. **Rename the subcommand.** `explain last` suggests narrative output. `trace last` or `replay last` better conveys \"here's the machine-readable execution log.\" (This is cosmetic but reinforces the JSON-first stance.)\n\n**Evidence that would change my mind:**\n\nShow me user research (even a Slack thread) where someone asks \"I want to understand why my last run did X\" and the answer is \"pipe this JSON to jq.\" If users want prose, not data, the current default is correct. But the roadmap provides no such evidence \u2014 it just assumes Markdown-first because it's more human.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary: Blocking Issues and Required Changes\n\n| Issue | Severity | Reviewer(s) | Fix |\n|-------|----------|-------------|-----|\n| PII redaction ships in Phase 5, not Phase 2 | **FATAL** | A (agreed), B (agreed) | Move Council TODO #4 to Phase 2, Step 2.3; add redaction test to Phase 2 exit gate |\n| Schema stability assumed, not proven | **FATAL** | A (agreed) | Phase 1: audit `.work-state.json` contract, add version check, or promote to `docs/contracts/` |\n| Council findings are non-blocking | **SYSTEMIC** | A (half-agreed), self | Change resolution gate (line 438) to block Phase 2 merge on BLOCKING items |\n| `--quiet` flag spec'd but untested | **BLOCKING** | Self | Add `--quiet` test to Phase 2 exit gate (line 297) |\n| \"No recent run\" error message leaks paths | **BLOCKING** | Self | Spec relative-path-only error messages in Phase 2 exit gate |\n| Renderer LOC budget is too tight | **HIGH** | Self (new), A (related) | Remove LOC constraint, split into sections/, add Phase 3 extensibility |\n| `--json` is secondary but should be primary | **MEDIUM** | Self | Flip default, add `--markdown` opt-in, or provide user-research justification |\n| `router.json` contract undefined | **MEDIUM** | A (agreed) | Phase 1: schema or explicit \"internal-only, may break\" warning |\n\n**Would I greenlight this?**\n\n**No, with two preconditions for reversal:**\n\n1. **Merge blocker 1:** PII redaction must be in Phase 2, not Phase 5. The council caught this; the roadmap ignored it. Fix that inversion.\n2. **Merge blocker 2:** Schema coupling must be audited in Phase 1. Either prove `.work-state.json` is versioned, or add monitoring to regress tests when it changes.\n\nIf those two move, the remaining issues (rollback criteria, renderer extensibility, JSON-first default) are refinements I'd accept in follow-on PRs. But the privacy + stability risks are unshippable.\n\n---\n\n## 6. What the Roadmap Does Well (Credit Where Due)\n\n- **Read-only constraint is bulletproof** (line 86, line 120). The no-network test (Phase 4.2, line 362) is exactly right.\n- **Schema-first approach** (Phase 1.1) is correct. The JSON Schema 2020-12 choice is forward-compatible.\n- **Cost-profile kill-switch** (Phase 2.4) is the right UX pattern for optional features.\n- **Phase gating is strong** \u2014 each phase has a clear exit gate (e.g., lines 171-175, 291-297).\n\nThe bones are good. The flesh (PII handling, schema coupling, council sequencing) needs surgery before ship.",
